[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue: the parser reads dates month first. That explains most
of its reports. It does not explain all of them. Read by tenant and by what the reporter wrote, the
ticket holds three different problems with three different fixes.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not "known, one fix queued". The queued parser fix closes one of the three. The other two keep
producing reports after it ships.
</Callout>

## What is in the ticket

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

The export comes in overlapping pages, so the same report can appear twice. Every count here is by
report id, once.

### `parser`: the shared parser reads month first

Ticket: [issue 1](https://github.com/jakubburkiewicz/what-it-shows/issues/1).

A tenant whose locale writes the day first (asked of the platform, not a list) gets its dates
flipped: 7 April comes out as 4 July. Reports reach the desk by app, by email and by form. This is
the problem #4471 describes.

### `hint`: booking-v3 tells day-first people to type month first

Ticket: [issue 2](https://github.com/jakubburkiewicz/what-it-shows/issues/2).

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under the date field. Day-first tenants on that form type what the
hint says, and the form then reads it their way. Only form reports from those tenants. The parser
fix does not touch this: the parser is right here, the hint is wrong.

### `one-day`: a date moved by exactly one day

Ticket: [issue 3](https://github.com/jakubburkiewicz/what-it-shows/issues/3).

The desk notes mark these reports as a one-day move. Nothing is swapped: "Tuesday 12 May" arrives as
"Monday 11 May"; one client in Lisbon got Tuesday and the same meeting in Toronto got Monday; one
invite arrived before the booking was made. Month-and-day swapping cannot produce that. The cause is
not established from these reports. A time zone or day boundary problem fits what they describe;
engineering has to confirm it.

The desk files these reports under `parser` or `hint` by tenant, and counts them there. They are
counted again under `one-day`, because a fix for the swap does not help them.

## Why one ticket looked right

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on the subject line, and the subjects
here are generic ("Date is wrong", "Wrong day on the invite"). Intake's browser guess (the
`tagged as` column below) does not separate the problems either: it disagrees with the
tenant-based cause in both groups.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The `hint` row is the one the old page flagged as "not this ticket". This table counts two causes
and files the one-day reports inside them, as the desk does.

## What to do

- File and fix the three problems separately. One ticket each, each with its own count (see the table).
- Merge on cause, not on subject: tenant locale, booking form and channel
  ([triage: duplicate reports](/kb/triage/dedupe) shows the check).
- Write a new decision record that replaces 0001. An accepted decision is never edited.

